msgid "**Login methods:** SSO only. The frontend is a public-client OIDC single-page app with no form and no local accounts, and the controller and recorder need the OIDC client, so OpenTalk has no other method and no break-glass account; ``meta/services.yml`` marks the ``sso`` key ``nocheck: login-methods`` instead of declaring a ``login.method`` block."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** SSO only. The frontend is a public-client OIDC single-page app with no form and no local accounts, and the controller and recorder need the OIDC client, so OpenTalk has no other method and no break-glass account; `meta/services.yml` marks the `sso` key `nocheck: login-methods` instead of declaring a `login.method` block."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** ``services.decidim.login.method`` declares ``sso``, ``native`` and ``registration``. ``sso`` sets the organization’s ``omniauth_settings_openid_connect_enabled`` (`files/ruby/configure_oidc.rb <./files/ruby/configure_oidc.rb>`__), which shows or hides the OpenID Connect entry. ``native`` and ``registration`` set the organization’s ``users_registration_mode`` (`files/ruby/ensure/registration_mode.rb <./files/ruby/ensure/registration_mode.rb>`__): ``enabled`` with both on, ``existing`` (local sign-in without sign-up) with ``native`` alone, and ``disabled`` (external accounts only, no password form, no password reset, no sign-up) with ``native`` off. Decidim has no LDAP or second factor of its own, and Keycloak accounts keep registering through OpenID Connect in every mode."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** ``services.erpnext.login.method.<method>.enabled`` in `meta/services.yml <./meta/services.yml>`__ declares ``sso``, ``ldap``, ``native``, ``2fa``, ``email`` and ``registration``. ``sso`` sets ``enable_social_login`` of the Keycloak Social Login Key (`oidc_settings.py <./files/python/apply/oidc_settings.py>`__) and ``ldap`` sets ``enabled`` of the LDAP Settings (`ldap_source.py <./files/python/apply/ldap_source.py>`__); `login/methods.py <./files/python/apply/login/methods.py>`__ sets the System Settings ``disable_user_pass_login`` (off while ``native`` is on), ``login_with_email_link`` (``email``), ``enable_two_factor_auth`` (``2fa``) and the Website Settings ``disable_signup`` (off while ``registration`` is on). Frappe asks for the second factor only from members of a Role whose ``Two Factor Authentication`` box is checked, so ``login.method.2fa.enforced`` (default ``true``) checks that box on the Role ``All``, which every user holds, while ``2fa`` is on, and clears it while ``2fa`` is on and ``enforced`` is off. While ``2fa`` is off, ``enable_two_factor_auth`` is off too, and the deploy clears the box, also one an admin checked. Frappe mails every account its first OTP setup link, so the box stays clear while ``web-app-email`` is not deployed, and the deploy prints why: enforcing without mail would lock every account out of the web login, Administrator included. **Lockout risk:** that hold checks only that ``web-app-email`` is deployed, not that Frappe’s outgoing Email Account delivers. Frappe v16 exempts no account, so an enforcement over an SMTP path that does not deliver locks every account out of the web login, Administrator included. The SMTP probe Odoo runs is not mirrored here, because the outgoing Email Account is configured after the login methods and its probe needs the Frappe v16 runtime. Break-glass for a locked-out Administrator: ``bench --site <site> execute frappe.db.set_value --args \"['Role', 'All', 'two_factor_auth', 0]\"`` inside the backend container clears the box until the next deploy, and ``services.erpnext.login.method.2fa.enforced: false`` in the inventory keeps it clear. The LDAP Settings reuse the email and password fields of the sign-in form, so with ``native`` off in the LDAP mode the form stays for the “Login with LDAP” button while Frappe refuses every password sign-in of a local account. Break-glass: ``disable_user_pass_login`` also refuses the Administrator password, so with ``native`` off the path is the token of the API bot (``Authorization: token <api_key>:<api_secret>``, a System Manager) or ``bench --site <site> browse --user Administrator`` inside the backend container, which prints a signed-in desk URL."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** ``services.espocrm.login.method`` declares ``sso``, ``ldap``, ``native`` and ``2fa`` (see `Login Methods <../../docs/contributing/design/iam/login.md>`__). EspoCRM runs one ``authenticationMethod`` at a time, so ```templates/env.j2`` <./templates/env.j2>`__ maps them onto it: ``Oidc`` while ``sso`` is on, else ``LDAP`` while ``ldap`` is on, else ``Espo`` (the local form). The switches travel as ``ESPOCRM_SEED_*`` keys, which ```files/php/seed/config.php`` <./files/php/seed/config.php>`__ writes on every deploy, so an inventory override reaches a running install. Under ``Oidc``, ``native`` drives ``oidcFallback``, which is off by default so the sign-in page shows only the Keycloak button, and ``oidcAllowRegularUserFallback`` stays unset, so a fallback form admits administrators only. ``2fa`` drives ``auth2FA`` (TOTP from the default ``auth2FAMethodList``) through the seeder key ``ESPOCRM_SEED_AUTH2_F_A``, and ``login.method.2fa.enforced`` (default ``true``) drives ``auth2FAForced`` through ``ESPOCRM_SEED_AUTH2_F_A_FORCED``, so a regular user without a second factor enrolls one at the next sign-in; while ``2fa`` is off the seed is not rendered and ``auth2FAForced`` stays as an admin set it; EspoCRM cannot force portal users and bypasses the second factor for an OIDC sign-in."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** ``services.fider.login.method`` declares ``sso`` and ``native``; Fider has no password form, LDAP or second factor of its own, and its email code sign-in is its local sign-in. ``sso`` sets the ``status`` of the Keycloak row in ``oauth_providers`` (``2`` lists “Continue with … SSO” on the sign-in modal, ``1`` hides it, `files/sql/configure-oidc-provider.sql <./files/sql/configure-oidc-provider.sql>`__). ``native`` sets the tenant setting ``is_email_auth_allowed`` (`files/sql/email-auth.sql <./files/sql/email-auth.sql>`__), which shows or removes the email code form: without SSO every account signs in by email code, with SSO the form is gone."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** ``services.grafana.login.method`` declares ``sso``, ``native`` and ``registration`` (see `Login Methods <../../docs/contributing/design/iam/login.md>`__). ``sso`` switches the ``auth.generic_oauth`` block of ``templates/env.j2`` with auto-login, ``native`` drives ``GF_AUTH_DISABLE_LOGIN_FORM`` (the local form is off while ``native`` is off) and ``registration`` drives ``GF_USERS_ALLOW_SIGN_UP``. Grafana OSS has no second factor of its own, so the role declares no ``2fa``, and it has no ``ldap`` service."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** ``services.jellyfin.login.method`` declares ``sso``, ``ldap`` and ``native``. Jellyfin has no setting that hides its own sign-in form, so `files/shell/configure-auth.sh <./files/shell/configure-auth.sh>`__ writes the sign-in page through the server branding: ``sso`` puts the “Sign in with Keycloak” button into the login disclaimer, and with ``ldap`` and ``native`` both off the custom CSS hides the password form, the user picker, “Manual Login”, “Quick Connect” and “Forgot Password”. Local and LDAP accounts share one form (shared form), so ``native: false`` in LDAP mode cannot keep other local accounts out. Jellyfin has no second factor, passkey or self-registration of its own. The CSS only shapes the web client: native Jellyfin apps keep their own sign-in screen, which the LDAP plugin and local accounts answer."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** ``services.jenkins.login.method`` declares ``sso``, ``ldap``, ``native`` and ``registration`` (see `Login Methods <../../docs/contributing/design/iam/login.md>`__). Jenkins runs one security realm, so ```templates/casc.yaml.j2`` <./templates/casc.yaml.j2>`__ maps them onto it: the ``oic`` realm while ``sso`` is on, else the ``ldap`` realm while ``ldap`` is on, else the ``local`` realm, which seeds the platform administrator while ``native`` is on and sets ``allowsSignup`` from ``registration``. Jenkins core has no second factor, so the role declares no ``2fa``."
msgstr ""

#, python-brace-format
msgid "**Login methods:** ``services.joomla.login.method`` declares ``sso``, ``ldap``, ``native``, ``2fa``, ``passkey`` and ``registration`` (see `Login Methods <../../docs/contributing/design/iam/login.md>`__). ``sso`` enables ``plg_system_keycloak``, which sends every guest to Keycloak. ``ldap`` enables ``plg_authentication_ldap`` (```tasks/10_ldap.yml`` <./tasks/10_ldap.yml>`__). ```tasks/11_login_methods.yml`` <./tasks/11_login_methods.yml>`__ runs ```files/php/login_methods.php`` <./files/php/login_methods.php>`__ on every deploy, which sets ``plg_authentication_joomla`` from ``native``, ``plg_system_webauthn`` (the passkey button) from ``passkey``, the ``totp``, ``yubikey``, ``webauthn`` and ``email`` plugins of the ``multifactorauth`` group from ``2fa``, and the com_users ``allowUserRegistration`` param from ``registration``. While ``2fa`` is on, ``login.method.2fa.enforced`` (default ``true``) sets the com_users ``forceMFAUserGroups`` param to the root user group ``Public`` (``JOOMLA_ROOT_USER_GROUP``), which every account inherits, Super Users included, so an account without a second factor sets one up at its next sign-in; ``enforced: false`` empties the list. While ``2fa`` is off the deploy disables every ``multifactorauth`` plugin, and Joomla would send a forced account to a methods page that offers nothing, so the same run empties the list, also one an admin chose, once no plugin of the ``multifactorauth`` group is enabled any more; while one still is, such as ``fixed`` or a third-party method an admin enabled, the list stays. Joomla enforces it only in the site and administrator applications, so the CLI and the Web Services API stay reachable. Break-glass for an account locked out by a forced list without an enabled method, with ``<prefix>`` the ``$dbprefix`` of ``configuration.php``: on MariaDB ``UPDATE <prefix>extensions SET params = JSON_SET(params, '$.forceMFAUserGroups', JSON_ARRAY()) WHERE type = 'component' AND element = 'com_users';``, on PostgreSQL ``UPDATE <prefix>extensions SET params = jsonb_set(params::jsonb, '{forceMFAUserGroups}', '[]')::text WHERE type = 'component' AND element = 'com_users';``, then ``php cli/joomla.php cache:clean``. Shared form: Joomla’s authentication plugins apply to every account, so Joomla cannot tell local from directory accounts; ``plg_authentication_joomla`` therefore stays on while ``ldap`` is on or the break-glass needs it, and ``native`` off removes local accounts from the form only when neither applies."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** ``services.litellm.login.method`` declares ``sso`` and ``native``. ``sso`` decides in `svc-ai-litellm <../svc-ai-litellm/vars/main.yml>`__ (``LITELLM_UI_SSO``) whether the gateway receives the ``GENERIC_*`` OIDC settings and the restricted ``ui_access_mode``; with them, the UI sends every unauthenticated visitor from ``/ui`` through ``/sso/key/generate`` straight to Keycloak and shows no password form. ``native`` is the ``UI_USERNAME`` and ``UI_PASSWORD`` form: LiteLLM has no switch that removes it, it is the sign-in page only while ``sso`` is off, and LiteLLM has no LDAP, second factor or self-registration of its own."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** ``services.mailu.login.method.<method>.enabled`` in `meta/services.yml <./meta/services.yml>`__ declares ``sso`` and ``native``. ``sso`` switches the OIDC fork on (``OIDC_ENABLED`` in `templates/env.j2 <./templates/env.j2>`__, driven by the ``sso`` service) and `templates/javascript.js.j2 <./templates/javascript.js.j2>`__ hides its ``openid-connect/auth`` link on ``/sso/login`` when the method is off. The fork has no setting that drops the local form, so with ``native`` off the same script hides the form of ``/sso/login``, which serves the Webmail and the Admin sign-in alike. Self-registration stays off in every mode (no domain enables ``signup_enabled``, ``DOMAIN_REGISTRATION`` is unset), and Mailu has no second factor of its own. Break-glass: ``/sso/login?fallback=local`` shows the form again for the platform administrator’s mailbox, which keeps its password and the global administrator flag; the Mailu API (``API_TOKEN``) and ``flask mailu`` in the admin container remain as well."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** ``services.mastodon.login.method`` declares ``sso``, ``native``, ``2fa`` and ``registration`` (see `Login Methods <../../docs/contributing/design/iam/login.md>`__). ``sso`` renders the ``OIDC_*`` block of ```templates/env.j2`` <./templates/env.j2>`__. With ``sso`` on, ``native`` drives ``OMNIAUTH_ONLY`` (``true`` while ``native`` is off, so the e-mail and password form and the sign-up link disappear); without ``sso`` the form is the only sign-in. ``registration`` sets the ``registrations_mode`` setting (``open`` or ``none``) through ```files/ruby/ensure/registrations_mode.rb`` <./files/ruby/ensure/registrations_mode.rb>`__ on every deploy (```tasks/07_login_methods.yml`` <./tasks/07_login_methods.yml>`__); a Keycloak sign-in creates its account in either mode. ``2fa`` covers Mastodon’s own second factor (TOTP, security keys), which has no setting to switch it off: while ``2fa`` is off, the ``javascript`` service of ```meta/services.yml`` <./meta/services.yml>`__ is on and injects ```templates/javascript.js.j2`` <./templates/javascript.js.j2>`__, which hides the two-factor and security-key entries of the settings. Accounts that already enrolled keep their second factor at sign-in. Mastodon has no setting that requires a second factor, so ``login.method.2fa.enforced`` only offers it here."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** ``services.matomo.login.method.*`` of `meta/services.yml <./meta/services.yml>`__ switches each sign-in method on its own. ``sso`` installs and activates RebelOIDC. ``native`` has no Matomo setting, so while it is off the injected `files/javascript/javascript.js <./files/javascript/javascript.js>`__ hides the password form, together with its “Lost your password?” link, and the login page shows only the Keycloak entry. ``2fa`` activates Matomo’s own TwoFactorAuth plugin while on and deactivates it while off; no account is forced to enroll. ``login.method.2fa.enforced`` defaults to ``false`` and has no effect in this role, ``true`` included, because the role renders no enforcement: TwoFactorAuth sends every request of an account it requires to set up a second factor to the setup page, API requests with a ``token_auth`` included, which would cut off the deploy’s own ``token_auth`` calls of `tasks/07_config.yml <./tasks/07_config.yml>`__. Matomo has no local self-registration."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** ``services.minio.login.method.<method>.enabled`` in `meta/services.yml <./meta/services.yml>`__ declares ``sso``, ``ldap`` and ``native``. ``sso`` registers Keycloak as the named OpenID IdP (`tasks/01_oidc.yml <./tasks/01_oidc.yml>`__), which serves the STS ``AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity`` tier. ``ldap`` renders the ``MINIO_IDENTITY_LDAP_*`` settings (`templates/env.j2 <./templates/env.j2>`__) and binds the policies to the LDAP role groups (`tasks/02_ldap.yml <./tasks/02_ldap.yml>`__), so with SSO on an LDAP account signs in through Keycloak’s LDAP federation instead. ``native`` has no MinIO switch: the root account (``MINIO_ROOT_USER``) always signs in through the Console form, and the Console form is shared between root and LDAP accounts. The pinned Console renders no SSO entry, so the role keeps that form visible in every mode. Break-glass: the root account through the Console form at ``/login``, or ``mc`` with the root credentials."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** ``services.moodle.login.method`` declares ``sso``, ``ldap``, ``native``, ``2fa`` and ``registration``, and ```tasks/05_login_methods.yml`` <./tasks/05_login_methods.yml>`__ converges them on every deploy. ``sso`` puts ``oidc`` into Moodle’s ``auth`` list and, while ``native`` is off, points ``alternateloginurl`` at ``auth/oidc/``, so ``/login/index.php`` hands every visitor to Keycloak. ``ldap`` puts ``ldap`` into the ``auth`` list; under SSO ``auth_ldap`` stays in the list as a directory sync source only, behind the redirect. ``native`` is Moodle’s always-enabled manual auth, served by the same form as LDAP accounts (shared form); with SSO it only decides whether ``alternateloginurl`` hides that form, and in LDAP mode the only manual account is the site administrator. ``2fa`` sets ``tool_mfa/enabled`` and ``factor_totp/enabled``. While it is on, ``login.method.2fa.enforced: true``, the role default, turns ``factor_nosetup`` off and ``factor_grace`` on with ``forcesetup``, so an account without a factor is sent to setup within the grace period and refused after it; ``enforced: false`` keeps ``factor_nosetup`` on, so accounts may enrol a TOTP factor while accounts without one pass. Every variant in ```meta/variants.yml`` <./meta/variants.yml>`__ with SSO off sets ``enforced: false``, so its automated logins skip enrollment. While ``2fa`` is off the deploy turns ``tool_mfa`` off, which leaves no factor to enforce, and leaves ``factor_grace`` as an admin set it. Enforcing does not cut off the MCP server on the pinned Moodle 5.3.0: a token request is not logged in when ``tool_mfa_after_config`` runs, and ``external_api::validate_context`` calls ``require_login`` with ``preventredirect``, for which ``\\tool_mfa\\manager::should_require_mfa`` returns ``NO_REDIRECT`` before its web-service branch. ``registration`` sets ``registerauth=email`` and adds ``email`` to the ``auth`` list. **Break-glass:** ``/login/index.php?loginredirect=0`` keeps the form reachable under SSO, but ``tasks/04_oidc.yml`` moves every account to ``auth_oidc``, so the administrator regains form access only through Moodle’s CLI in the container (``admin/cli/cfg.php``, ``admin/cli/reset_password.php`` after switching its ``auth`` back to ``manual``)."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** ``services.odoo.login.method.*`` of ```meta/services.yml`` <./meta/services.yml>`__ switches each sign-in method on its own; ```tasks/08_login_methods.yml`` <./tasks/08_login_methods.yml>`__ and ```tasks/05_oidc.yml`` <./tasks/05_oidc.yml>`__ apply the database switches."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** ``services.opencloud.login.method`` declares ``sso``, ``ldap`` and ``native``. ``sso`` renders the ``OC_OIDC_ISSUER`` block of ``templates/env.j2``, which points OpenCloud Web and the proxy at Keycloak. ``ldap`` and ``native`` both keep the built-in identity provider running (``OC_EXCLUDE_RUN_SERVICES=idp`` is set only while both are off); it authenticates against the directory when ``svc-db-openldap`` is deployed and against the built-in IDM accounts otherwise, so LDAP mode admits no local account. OpenCloud has no own second factor and no self-registration. **Break-glass:** with SSO on no browser path admits the built-in ``admin`` account; administer the instance through the ``opencloud`` CLI inside the container, or set ``login.method.sso.enabled: false`` and ``login.method.native.enabled: true`` in the inventory and redeploy to get the built-in sign-in back."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** ``services.peertube.login.method.<method>.enabled`` in `meta/services.yml <./meta/services.yml>`__ declares ``sso``, ``native``, ``2fa`` and ``registration``. ``registration`` sets ``PEERTUBE_SIGNUP_ENABLED`` and ``sso`` together with ``native`` sets ``PEERTUBE_CLIENT_MENU_LOGIN_REDIRECT_ON_SINGLE_EXTERNAL_AUTH``, so the header login link goes straight to Keycloak while the OIDC entry is the only one (`templates/env.j2 <./templates/env.j2>`__). PeerTube has no setting that drops the local form, the OIDC entry or the TOTP second factor, so `templates/javascript.js.j2 <./templates/javascript.js.j2>`__ hides the ``/login`` form while ``native`` is off, the “Sign in with” block while ``sso`` is off and the two-factor section of the account settings while ``2fa`` is off; PeerTube has no setting that requires its TOTP second factor, so ``login.method.2fa.enforced`` only offers it for enrollment here. Break-glass: ``/login?fallback=local`` shows the form again for the local ``root`` account; the OAuth password grant of ``/api/v1/users/token`` that `tasks/03_admin.yml <./tasks/03_admin.yml>`__ uses stays open as well."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** ``services.pgadmin.login.method.<method>.enabled`` in `meta/services.yml <./meta/services.yml>`__ declares ``sso``, ``native`` and ``2fa``. ``sso`` adds ``oauth2`` and ``native`` adds ``internal`` to ``PGADMIN_CONFIG_AUTHENTICATION_SOURCES`` (``PGADMIN_AUTHENTICATION_SOURCES`` in `vars/main.yml <./vars/main.yml>`__); pgAdmin hides the email and password form, the forgotten-password link and the Login button whenever ``internal`` is missing and refuses an internal sign-in server-side. ``2fa`` sets ``PGADMIN_CONFIG_MFA_ENABLED``, which offers the email and authenticator second factor for enrollment, and ``login.method.2fa.enforced`` (default ``true``) sets ``PGADMIN_CONFIG_MFA_FORCE_REGISTRATION``, which makes an account without a second factor register one at its next sign-in. pgAdmin has no self-registration. Break-glass: with SSO on the internal administrator (``PGADMIN_DEFAULT_EMAIL``) has no web sign-in, so the path is the CLI inside the container, ``/venv/bin/python3 /pgadmin4/setup.py update-external-user <username> --auth-source oauth2 --admin`` to promote an OAuth2 account, or setting ``applications.web-app-pgadmin.services.pgadmin.login.method.native.enabled: true`` and redeploying to bring the internal form back."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** ``services.pixelfed.login.method`` declares ``sso``, ``native``, ``2fa`` and ``registration``; Pixelfed has no LDAP or passkey. ``sso`` sets ``PF_OIDC_ENABLED``, which shows “Sign-in with OIDC”, and ``registration`` sets ``OPEN_REGISTRATION``, which shows the sign-up link (`templates/env.j2 <./templates/env.j2>`__). Pixelfed has no setting for its password form or its two-factor enrollment, so `templates/javascript.js.j2 <./templates/javascript.js.j2>`__, injected through the ``javascript`` service whenever ``native`` or ``2fa`` is off, hides the email and password form on ``/login`` while ``native`` is off and the “Enable two-factor authentication” entry under ``/settings/security`` while ``2fa`` is off; both still answer on the server. Pixelfed has no setting that requires a second factor, so ``login.method.2fa.enforced`` only offers it here."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** ``services.pretix.login.method.*`` of `meta/services.yml <./meta/services.yml>`__ switches each sign-in method on its own. ``sso`` appends the pretix-oidc backend to ``PRETIX_PRETIX_AUTH_BACKENDS``; ``registration`` sets ``PRETIX_PRETIX_REGISTRATION``; ``native`` sets ``PRETIX_PRETIX_PASSWORD_RESET``, and since pretix has no setting that hides its native form, `templates/javascript.js.j2 <./templates/javascript.js.j2>`__ hides the native tab, form, password reset and registration links on ``/control/login`` while ``native`` is off; ``2fa`` has no pretix setting either, so the same script hides the “Two-factor authentication” enrollment entry of the account settings while it is off. While ``2fa`` is on, ``login.method.2fa.enforced`` (default ``true``) renders ``PRETIX_PRETIX_OBLIGATORY_2FA=True`` (``obligatory_2fa`` of the ``[pretix]`` section). With SSO on, the sign-in page shows only the Keycloak entry."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** ``services.semaphore.login.method.*`` of `meta/services.yml <./meta/services.yml>`__ switches each sign-in method on its own. ``sso`` renders the Keycloak provider through ``SEMAPHORE_OIDC_PROVIDERS``; ``ldap`` sets ``SEMAPHORE_LDAP_ENABLE``; ``native`` and ``ldap`` share one form, which ``SEMAPHORE_PASSWORD_LOGIN_DISABLED`` hides while both are off; ``2fa`` offers TOTP enrollment through ``SEMAPHORE_TOTP_ENABLED``, and Semaphore has no setting that requires it, so ``login.method.2fa.enforced`` only offers it here. With SSO on, the sign-in page shows only the Keycloak entry and LDAP accounts sign in through Keycloak’s LDAP federation."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** ``services.shopware.login.method.*`` of `meta/services.yml <./meta/services.yml>`__ switches each sign-in method of the administration on its own. ``sso`` sets the ``login`` flag of the AdminOpenAuth Keycloak client in ```files/php/oidc/client.php`` <./files/php/oidc/client.php>`__. ``native`` sets AdminOpenAuth’s ``denyPasswordLogin`` while it is off, which hides the password form and its “forgot password” link; ``enableAutoRedirect`` stays off, so the login page shows the Keycloak entry instead of jumping to Keycloak. The administration has no LDAP sign-in, no second factor and no self-registration of its own."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** ``services.suitecrm.login.method`` declares ``sso``, ``ldap``, ``native`` and ``2fa`` (see `Login Methods <../../docs/contributing/design/iam/login.md>`__). SuiteCRM runs one ``AUTH_TYPE`` at a time, so ```templates/env.j2`` <./templates/env.j2>`__ maps them onto it: ``saml`` while ``sso`` is on, else ``ldap`` while ``ldap`` is on, else ``native`` while ``native`` is on (SuiteCRM’s own ``.env`` falls back to ``native`` when every method is off). ``2fa`` covers SuiteCRM’s TOTP second factor (``scheb/2fa-totp``, enabled in its ``config/packages/scheb_2fa.yaml``), which has no switch the role can set safely: while ``2fa`` is off, the ``javascript`` service of ```meta/services.yml`` <./meta/services.yml>`__ is on and injects ```templates/javascript.js.j2`` <./templates/javascript.js.j2>`__, which hides the two-factor enrollment view (``scrm-2fa``, ``#two-factor``). Accounts that already enrolled keep their second factor at sign-in. SuiteCRM core has no setting that requires a second factor, so ``login.method.2fa.enforced`` only offers it here."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** ``services.wordpress.login.method`` declares ``sso``, ``native`` and ``registration`` (see `Login Methods <../../docs/contributing/design/iam/login.md>`__). ``sso`` enables the ``daggerhart-openid-connect-generic`` addon. With ``sso`` on, ``native`` sets the addon’s ``login_type``: ``auto`` (the default, ``wp-login.php`` redirects to Keycloak) while ``native`` is off, ``button`` (form plus Keycloak button) while it is on; without ``sso`` the WordPress form is the only sign-in. ``registration`` sets ``users_can_register``, or the network option ``registration`` (``user`` or ``none``) under Multisite, through WP-CLI in ```tasks/07_manager_ops.yml`` <./tasks/07_manager_ops.yml>`__. WordPress core has no second factor and the role no ``ldap`` service, so neither is declared."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** ``services.xwiki.login.method`` declares ``sso``, ``ldap``, ``native`` and ``registration``. ``sso`` renders ``security.authentication.authService=oidc`` into ``xwiki.properties``, so the login action hands every visitor to Keycloak. ``ldap`` renders ``authService=ldap``, which only applies while OIDC is off. ``native`` sets ``xwiki.authentication.ldap.trylocal`` in LDAP mode, so with its default ``false`` only directory accounts and the built-in ``superadmin`` pass the form; without SSO and LDAP the standard XWiki form serves the local accounts. ``registration`` keeps or removes a global rights object that denies the ``register`` right to ``XWiki.XWikiGuest`` (``tasks/08_registration.yml``), which hides the Register entry and refuses ``XWiki.XWikiRegister``. **Break-glass:** with SSO on, ``/bin/login/XWiki/XWikiLogin?oidc.skipped=true`` opens the local form (the OIDC authenticator honours it while ``oidc.tryLocal`` keeps its default ``true``), and ``superadmin`` with ``credentials.superadminpassword`` signs in there or through REST basic auth."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** ``services.zammad.login.method.<method>.enabled`` in `meta/services.yml <./meta/services.yml>`__ declares ``sso``, ``ldap``, ``native``, ``2fa`` and ``registration``, and `files/ruby/apply/login_methods.rb <./files/ruby/apply/login_methods.rb>`__ writes them into Zammad’s settings at every deploy: ``sso`` sets ``auth_openid_connect``, ``native`` or ``ldap`` sets ``user_show_password_login``, ``registration`` sets ``user_create_account``, and ``2fa`` sets the authenticator app and security key methods; while it is on, ``login.method.2fa.enforced`` (default ``true``) puts every active role into ``two_factor_authentication_enforce_role_ids``, and ``enforced: false`` empties the list so the second factor is only offered; while ``2fa`` is off both methods are off, so an enforcement is dormant (``Auth::TwoFactor#user_setup_required?`` starts with ``enabled? &&``), and the deploy empties the list, also one an admin set, so it does not wake up once an admin enables a method again. One form serves local and LDAP accounts and Zammad cannot tell them apart, so the form is shared: with ``native`` off in the LDAP mode a local account still signs in through it. The LDAP import keeps running with the ``ldap`` service, because it carries the role mapping of the directory users. Break-glass: with the password form hidden, the sign-in page links ``#admin_password_auth``, which mails an administrator a one-time password-login link; ``POST /api/v1/signin`` keeps accepting the local administrator accounts (the API bot of ``ZAMMAD_API_BOT_USER``, the wizard administrator), as does Basic auth on the REST API."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** `services.decidim.login.method` declares `sso`, `native` and `registration`. `sso` sets the organization's `omniauth_settings_openid_connect_enabled` ([files/ruby/configure_oidc.rb](./files/ruby/configure_oidc.rb)), which shows or hides the OpenID Connect entry. `native` and `registration` set the organization's `users_registration_mode` ([files/ruby/ensure/registration_mode.rb](./files/ruby/ensure/registration_mode.rb)): `enabled` with both on, `existing` (local sign-in without sign-up) with `native` alone, and `disabled` (external accounts only, no password form, no password reset, no sign-up) with `native` off. Decidim has no LDAP or second factor of its own, and Keycloak accounts keep registering through OpenID Connect in every mode."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** `services.erpnext.login.method.<method>.enabled` in [meta/services.yml](./meta/services.yml) declares `sso`, `ldap`, `native`, `2fa`, `email` and `registration`. `sso` sets `enable_social_login` of the Keycloak Social Login Key ([oidc_settings.py](./files/python/apply/oidc_settings.py)) and `ldap` sets `enabled` of the LDAP Settings ([ldap_source.py](./files/python/apply/ldap_source.py)); [login/methods.py](./files/python/apply/login/methods.py) sets the System Settings `disable_user_pass_login` (off while `native` is on), `login_with_email_link` (`email`), `enable_two_factor_auth` (`2fa`) and the Website Settings `disable_signup` (off while `registration` is on). Frappe asks for the second factor only from members of a Role whose `Two Factor Authentication` box is checked, so `login.method.2fa.enforced` (default `true`) checks that box on the Role `All`, which every user holds, while `2fa` is on, and clears it while `2fa` is on and `enforced` is off. While `2fa` is off, `enable_two_factor_auth` is off too, and the deploy clears the box, also one an admin checked. Frappe mails every account its first OTP setup link, so the box stays clear while `web-app-email` is not deployed, and the deploy prints why: enforcing without mail would lock every account out of the web login, Administrator included. **Lockout risk:** that hold checks only that `web-app-email` is deployed, not that Frappe's outgoing Email Account delivers. Frappe v16 exempts no account, so an enforcement over an SMTP path that does not deliver locks every account out of the web login, Administrator included. The SMTP probe Odoo runs is not mirrored here, because the outgoing Email Account is configured after the login methods and its probe needs the Frappe v16 runtime. Break-glass for a locked-out Administrator: `bench --site <site> execute frappe.db.set_value --args \"['Role', 'All', 'two_factor_auth', 0]\"` inside the backend container clears the box until the next deploy, and `services.erpnext.login.method.2fa.enforced: false` in the inventory keeps it clear. The LDAP Settings reuse the email and password fields of the sign-in form, so with `native` off in the LDAP mode the form stays for the \"Login with LDAP\" button while Frappe refuses every password sign-in of a local account. Break-glass: `disable_user_pass_login` also refuses the Administrator password, so with `native` off the path is the token of the API bot (`Authorization: token <api_key>:<api_secret>`, a System Manager) or `bench --site <site> browse --user Administrator` inside the backend container, which prints a signed-in desk URL."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** `services.espocrm.login.method` declares `sso`, `ldap`, `native` and `2fa` (see [Login Methods](../../docs/contributing/design/iam/login.md)). EspoCRM runs one `authenticationMethod` at a time, so [`templates/env.j2`](./templates/env.j2) maps them onto it: `Oidc` while `sso` is on, else `LDAP` while `ldap` is on, else `Espo` (the local form). The switches travel as `ESPOCRM_SEED_*` keys, which [`files/php/seed/config.php`](./files/php/seed/config.php) writes on every deploy, so an inventory override reaches a running install. Under `Oidc`, `native` drives `oidcFallback`, which is off by default so the sign-in page shows only the Keycloak button, and `oidcAllowRegularUserFallback` stays unset, so a fallback form admits administrators only. `2fa` drives `auth2FA` (TOTP from the default `auth2FAMethodList`) through the seeder key `ESPOCRM_SEED_AUTH2_F_A`, and `login.method.2fa.enforced` (default `true`) drives `auth2FAForced` through `ESPOCRM_SEED_AUTH2_F_A_FORCED`, so a regular user without a second factor enrolls one at the next sign-in; while `2fa` is off the seed is not rendered and `auth2FAForced` stays as an admin set it; EspoCRM cannot force portal users and bypasses the second factor for an OIDC sign-in."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** `services.fider.login.method` declares `sso` and `native`; Fider has no password form, LDAP or second factor of its own, and its email code sign-in is its local sign-in. `sso` sets the `status` of the Keycloak row in `oauth_providers` (`2` lists \"Continue with … SSO\" on the sign-in modal, `1` hides it, [files/sql/configure-oidc-provider.sql](./files/sql/configure-oidc-provider.sql)). `native` sets the tenant setting `is_email_auth_allowed` ([files/sql/email-auth.sql](./files/sql/email-auth.sql)), which shows or removes the email code form: without SSO every account signs in by email code, with SSO the form is gone."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** `services.grafana.login.method` declares `sso`, `native` and `registration` (see [Login Methods](../../docs/contributing/design/iam/login.md)). `sso` switches the `auth.generic_oauth` block of `templates/env.j2` with auto-login, `native` drives `GF_AUTH_DISABLE_LOGIN_FORM` (the local form is off while `native` is off) and `registration` drives `GF_USERS_ALLOW_SIGN_UP`. Grafana OSS has no second factor of its own, so the role declares no `2fa`, and it has no `ldap` service."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** `services.jellyfin.login.method` declares `sso`, `ldap` and `native`. Jellyfin has no setting that hides its own sign-in form, so [files/shell/configure-auth.sh](./files/shell/configure-auth.sh) writes the sign-in page through the server branding: `sso` puts the \"Sign in with Keycloak\" button into the login disclaimer, and with `ldap` and `native` both off the custom CSS hides the password form, the user picker, \"Manual Login\", \"Quick Connect\" and \"Forgot Password\". Local and LDAP accounts share one form (shared form), so `native: false` in LDAP mode cannot keep other local accounts out. Jellyfin has no second factor, passkey or self-registration of its own. The CSS only shapes the web client: native Jellyfin apps keep their own sign-in screen, which the LDAP plugin and local accounts answer."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** `services.jenkins.login.method` declares `sso`, `ldap`, `native` and `registration` (see [Login Methods](../../docs/contributing/design/iam/login.md)). Jenkins runs one security realm, so [`templates/casc.yaml.j2`](./templates/casc.yaml.j2) maps them onto it: the `oic` realm while `sso` is on, else the `ldap` realm while `ldap` is on, else the `local` realm, which seeds the platform administrator while `native` is on and sets `allowsSignup` from `registration`. Jenkins core has no second factor, so the role declares no `2fa`."
msgstr ""

#, python-brace-format
msgid "**Login methods:** `services.joomla.login.method` declares `sso`, `ldap`, `native`, `2fa`, `passkey` and `registration` (see [Login Methods](../../docs/contributing/design/iam/login.md)). `sso` enables `plg_system_keycloak`, which sends every guest to Keycloak. `ldap` enables `plg_authentication_ldap` ([`tasks/10_ldap.yml`](./tasks/10_ldap.yml)). [`tasks/11_login_methods.yml`](./tasks/11_login_methods.yml) runs [`files/php/login_methods.php`](./files/php/login_methods.php) on every deploy, which sets `plg_authentication_joomla` from `native`, `plg_system_webauthn` (the passkey button) from `passkey`, the `totp`, `yubikey`, `webauthn` and `email` plugins of the `multifactorauth` group from `2fa`, and the com_users `allowUserRegistration` param from `registration`. While `2fa` is on, `login.method.2fa.enforced` (default `true`) sets the com_users `forceMFAUserGroups` param to the root user group `Public` (`JOOMLA_ROOT_USER_GROUP`), which every account inherits, Super Users included, so an account without a second factor sets one up at its next sign-in; `enforced: false` empties the list. While `2fa` is off the deploy disables every `multifactorauth` plugin, and Joomla would send a forced account to a methods page that offers nothing, so the same run empties the list, also one an admin chose, once no plugin of the `multifactorauth` group is enabled any more; while one still is, such as `fixed` or a third-party method an admin enabled, the list stays. Joomla enforces it only in the site and administrator applications, so the CLI and the Web Services API stay reachable. Break-glass for an account locked out by a forced list without an enabled method, with `<prefix>` the `$dbprefix` of `configuration.php`: on MariaDB `UPDATE <prefix>extensions SET params = JSON_SET(params, '$.forceMFAUserGroups', JSON_ARRAY()) WHERE type = 'component' AND element = 'com_users';`, on PostgreSQL `UPDATE <prefix>extensions SET params = jsonb_set(params::jsonb, '{forceMFAUserGroups}', '[]')::text WHERE type = 'component' AND element = 'com_users';`, then `php cli/joomla.php cache:clean`. Shared form: Joomla's authentication plugins apply to every account, so Joomla cannot tell local from directory accounts; `plg_authentication_joomla` therefore stays on while `ldap` is on or the break-glass needs it, and `native` off removes local accounts from the form only when neither applies."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** `services.litellm.login.method` declares `sso` and `native`. `sso` decides in [svc-ai-litellm](../svc-ai-litellm/vars/main.yml) (`LITELLM_UI_SSO`) whether the gateway receives the `GENERIC_*` OIDC settings and the restricted `ui_access_mode`; with them, the UI sends every unauthenticated visitor from `/ui` through `/sso/key/generate` straight to Keycloak and shows no password form. `native` is the `UI_USERNAME` and `UI_PASSWORD` form: LiteLLM has no switch that removes it, it is the sign-in page only while `sso` is off, and LiteLLM has no LDAP, second factor or self-registration of its own."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** `services.mailu.login.method.<method>.enabled` in [meta/services.yml](./meta/services.yml) declares `sso` and `native`. `sso` switches the OIDC fork on (`OIDC_ENABLED` in [templates/env.j2](./templates/env.j2), driven by the `sso` service) and [templates/javascript.js.j2](./templates/javascript.js.j2) hides its `openid-connect/auth` link on `/sso/login` when the method is off. The fork has no setting that drops the local form, so with `native` off the same script hides the form of `/sso/login`, which serves the Webmail and the Admin sign-in alike. Self-registration stays off in every mode (no domain enables `signup_enabled`, `DOMAIN_REGISTRATION` is unset), and Mailu has no second factor of its own. Break-glass: `/sso/login?fallback=local` shows the form again for the platform administrator's mailbox, which keeps its password and the global administrator flag; the Mailu API (`API_TOKEN`) and `flask mailu` in the admin container remain as well."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** `services.mastodon.login.method` declares `sso`, `native`, `2fa` and `registration` (see [Login Methods](../../docs/contributing/design/iam/login.md)). `sso` renders the `OIDC_*` block of [`templates/env.j2`](./templates/env.j2). With `sso` on, `native` drives `OMNIAUTH_ONLY` (`true` while `native` is off, so the e-mail and password form and the sign-up link disappear); without `sso` the form is the only sign-in. `registration` sets the `registrations_mode` setting (`open` or `none`) through [`files/ruby/ensure/registrations_mode.rb`](./files/ruby/ensure/registrations_mode.rb) on every deploy ([`tasks/07_login_methods.yml`](./tasks/07_login_methods.yml)); a Keycloak sign-in creates its account in either mode. `2fa` covers Mastodon's own second factor (TOTP, security keys), which has no setting to switch it off: while `2fa` is off, the `javascript` service of [`meta/services.yml`](./meta/services.yml) is on and injects [`templates/javascript.js.j2`](./templates/javascript.js.j2), which hides the two-factor and security-key entries of the settings. Accounts that already enrolled keep their second factor at sign-in. Mastodon has no setting that requires a second factor, so `login.method.2fa.enforced` only offers it here."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** `services.matomo.login.method.*` of [meta/services.yml](./meta/services.yml) switches each sign-in method on its own. `sso` installs and activates RebelOIDC. `native` has no Matomo setting, so while it is off the injected [files/javascript/javascript.js](./files/javascript/javascript.js) hides the password form, together with its \"Lost your password?\" link, and the login page shows only the Keycloak entry. `2fa` activates Matomo's own TwoFactorAuth plugin while on and deactivates it while off; no account is forced to enroll. `login.method.2fa.enforced` defaults to `false` and has no effect in this role, `true` included, because the role renders no enforcement: TwoFactorAuth sends every request of an account it requires to set up a second factor to the setup page, API requests with a `token_auth` included, which would cut off the deploy's own `token_auth` calls of [tasks/07_config.yml](./tasks/07_config.yml). Matomo has no local self-registration."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** `services.minio.login.method.<method>.enabled` in [meta/services.yml](./meta/services.yml) declares `sso`, `ldap` and `native`. `sso` registers Keycloak as the named OpenID IdP ([tasks/01_oidc.yml](./tasks/01_oidc.yml)), which serves the STS `AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity` tier. `ldap` renders the `MINIO_IDENTITY_LDAP_*` settings ([templates/env.j2](./templates/env.j2)) and binds the policies to the LDAP role groups ([tasks/02_ldap.yml](./tasks/02_ldap.yml)), so with SSO on an LDAP account signs in through Keycloak's LDAP federation instead. `native` has no MinIO switch: the root account (`MINIO_ROOT_USER`) always signs in through the Console form, and the Console form is shared between root and LDAP accounts. The pinned Console renders no SSO entry, so the role keeps that form visible in every mode. Break-glass: the root account through the Console form at `/login`, or `mc` with the root credentials."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** `services.moodle.login.method` declares `sso`, `ldap`, `native`, `2fa` and `registration`, and [`tasks/05_login_methods.yml`](./tasks/05_login_methods.yml) converges them on every deploy. `sso` puts `oidc` into Moodle's `auth` list and, while `native` is off, points `alternateloginurl` at `auth/oidc/`, so `/login/index.php` hands every visitor to Keycloak. `ldap` puts `ldap` into the `auth` list; under SSO `auth_ldap` stays in the list as a directory sync source only, behind the redirect. `native` is Moodle's always-enabled manual auth, served by the same form as LDAP accounts (shared form); with SSO it only decides whether `alternateloginurl` hides that form, and in LDAP mode the only manual account is the site administrator. `2fa` sets `tool_mfa/enabled` and `factor_totp/enabled`. While it is on, `login.method.2fa.enforced: true`, the role default, turns `factor_nosetup` off and `factor_grace` on with `forcesetup`, so an account without a factor is sent to setup within the grace period and refused after it; `enforced: false` keeps `factor_nosetup` on, so accounts may enrol a TOTP factor while accounts without one pass. Every variant in [`meta/variants.yml`](./meta/variants.yml) with SSO off sets `enforced: false`, so its automated logins skip enrollment. While `2fa` is off the deploy turns `tool_mfa` off, which leaves no factor to enforce, and leaves `factor_grace` as an admin set it. Enforcing does not cut off the MCP server on the pinned Moodle 5.3.0: a token request is not logged in when `tool_mfa_after_config` runs, and `external_api::validate_context` calls `require_login` with `preventredirect`, for which `\\tool_mfa\\manager::should_require_mfa` returns `NO_REDIRECT` before its web-service branch. `registration` sets `registerauth=email` and adds `email` to the `auth` list. **Break-glass:** `/login/index.php?loginredirect=0` keeps the form reachable under SSO, but `tasks/04_oidc.yml` moves every account to `auth_oidc`, so the administrator regains form access only through Moodle's CLI in the container (`admin/cli/cfg.php`, `admin/cli/reset_password.php` after switching its `auth` back to `manual`)."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** `services.odoo.login.method.*` of [`meta/services.yml`](./meta/services.yml) switches each sign-in method on its own; [`tasks/08_login_methods.yml`](./tasks/08_login_methods.yml) and [`tasks/05_oidc.yml`](./tasks/05_oidc.yml) apply the database switches."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** `services.opencloud.login.method` declares `sso`, `ldap` and `native`. `sso` renders the `OC_OIDC_ISSUER` block of `templates/env.j2`, which points OpenCloud Web and the proxy at Keycloak. `ldap` and `native` both keep the built-in identity provider running (`OC_EXCLUDE_RUN_SERVICES=idp` is set only while both are off); it authenticates against the directory when `svc-db-openldap` is deployed and against the built-in IDM accounts otherwise, so LDAP mode admits no local account. OpenCloud has no own second factor and no self-registration. **Break-glass:** with SSO on no browser path admits the built-in `admin` account; administer the instance through the `opencloud` CLI inside the container, or set `login.method.sso.enabled: false` and `login.method.native.enabled: true` in the inventory and redeploy to get the built-in sign-in back."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** `services.peertube.login.method.<method>.enabled` in [meta/services.yml](./meta/services.yml) declares `sso`, `native`, `2fa` and `registration`. `registration` sets `PEERTUBE_SIGNUP_ENABLED` and `sso` together with `native` sets `PEERTUBE_CLIENT_MENU_LOGIN_REDIRECT_ON_SINGLE_EXTERNAL_AUTH`, so the header login link goes straight to Keycloak while the OIDC entry is the only one ([templates/env.j2](./templates/env.j2)). PeerTube has no setting that drops the local form, the OIDC entry or the TOTP second factor, so [templates/javascript.js.j2](./templates/javascript.js.j2) hides the `/login` form while `native` is off, the \"Sign in with\" block while `sso` is off and the two-factor section of the account settings while `2fa` is off; PeerTube has no setting that requires its TOTP second factor, so `login.method.2fa.enforced` only offers it for enrollment here. Break-glass: `/login?fallback=local` shows the form again for the local `root` account; the OAuth password grant of `/api/v1/users/token` that [tasks/03_admin.yml](./tasks/03_admin.yml) uses stays open as well."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** `services.pgadmin.login.method.<method>.enabled` in [meta/services.yml](./meta/services.yml) declares `sso`, `native` and `2fa`. `sso` adds `oauth2` and `native` adds `internal` to `PGADMIN_CONFIG_AUTHENTICATION_SOURCES` (`PGADMIN_AUTHENTICATION_SOURCES` in [vars/main.yml](./vars/main.yml)); pgAdmin hides the email and password form, the forgotten-password link and the Login button whenever `internal` is missing and refuses an internal sign-in server-side. `2fa` sets `PGADMIN_CONFIG_MFA_ENABLED`, which offers the email and authenticator second factor for enrollment, and `login.method.2fa.enforced` (default `true`) sets `PGADMIN_CONFIG_MFA_FORCE_REGISTRATION`, which makes an account without a second factor register one at its next sign-in. pgAdmin has no self-registration. Break-glass: with SSO on the internal administrator (`PGADMIN_DEFAULT_EMAIL`) has no web sign-in, so the path is the CLI inside the container, `/venv/bin/python3 /pgadmin4/setup.py update-external-user <username> --auth-source oauth2 --admin` to promote an OAuth2 account, or setting `applications.web-app-pgadmin.services.pgadmin.login.method.native.enabled: true` and redeploying to bring the internal form back."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** `services.pixelfed.login.method` declares `sso`, `native`, `2fa` and `registration`; Pixelfed has no LDAP or passkey. `sso` sets `PF_OIDC_ENABLED`, which shows \"Sign-in with OIDC\", and `registration` sets `OPEN_REGISTRATION`, which shows the sign-up link ([templates/env.j2](./templates/env.j2)). Pixelfed has no setting for its password form or its two-factor enrollment, so [templates/javascript.js.j2](./templates/javascript.js.j2), injected through the `javascript` service whenever `native` or `2fa` is off, hides the email and password form on `/login` while `native` is off and the \"Enable two-factor authentication\" entry under `/settings/security` while `2fa` is off; both still answer on the server. Pixelfed has no setting that requires a second factor, so `login.method.2fa.enforced` only offers it here."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** `services.pretix.login.method.*` of [meta/services.yml](./meta/services.yml) switches each sign-in method on its own. `sso` appends the pretix-oidc backend to `PRETIX_PRETIX_AUTH_BACKENDS`; `registration` sets `PRETIX_PRETIX_REGISTRATION`; `native` sets `PRETIX_PRETIX_PASSWORD_RESET`, and since pretix has no setting that hides its native form, [templates/javascript.js.j2](./templates/javascript.js.j2) hides the native tab, form, password reset and registration links on `/control/login` while `native` is off; `2fa` has no pretix setting either, so the same script hides the \"Two-factor authentication\" enrollment entry of the account settings while it is off. While `2fa` is on, `login.method.2fa.enforced` (default `true`) renders `PRETIX_PRETIX_OBLIGATORY_2FA=True` (`obligatory_2fa` of the `[pretix]` section). With SSO on, the sign-in page shows only the Keycloak entry."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** `services.semaphore.login.method.*` of [meta/services.yml](./meta/services.yml) switches each sign-in method on its own. `sso` renders the Keycloak provider through `SEMAPHORE_OIDC_PROVIDERS`; `ldap` sets `SEMAPHORE_LDAP_ENABLE`; `native` and `ldap` share one form, which `SEMAPHORE_PASSWORD_LOGIN_DISABLED` hides while both are off; `2fa` offers TOTP enrollment through `SEMAPHORE_TOTP_ENABLED`, and Semaphore has no setting that requires it, so `login.method.2fa.enforced` only offers it here. With SSO on, the sign-in page shows only the Keycloak entry and LDAP accounts sign in through Keycloak's LDAP federation."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** `services.shopware.login.method.*` of [meta/services.yml](./meta/services.yml) switches each sign-in method of the administration on its own. `sso` sets the `login` flag of the AdminOpenAuth Keycloak client in [`files/php/oidc/client.php`](./files/php/oidc/client.php). `native` sets AdminOpenAuth's `denyPasswordLogin` while it is off, which hides the password form and its \"forgot password\" link; `enableAutoRedirect` stays off, so the login page shows the Keycloak entry instead of jumping to Keycloak. The administration has no LDAP sign-in, no second factor and no self-registration of its own."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** `services.suitecrm.login.method` declares `sso`, `ldap`, `native` and `2fa` (see [Login Methods](../../docs/contributing/design/iam/login.md)). SuiteCRM runs one `AUTH_TYPE` at a time, so [`templates/env.j2`](./templates/env.j2) maps them onto it: `saml` while `sso` is on, else `ldap` while `ldap` is on, else `native` while `native` is on (SuiteCRM's own `.env` falls back to `native` when every method is off). `2fa` covers SuiteCRM's TOTP second factor (`scheb/2fa-totp`, enabled in its `config/packages/scheb_2fa.yaml`), which has no switch the role can set safely: while `2fa` is off, the `javascript` service of [`meta/services.yml`](./meta/services.yml) is on and injects [`templates/javascript.js.j2`](./templates/javascript.js.j2), which hides the two-factor enrollment view (`scrm-2fa`, `#two-factor`). Accounts that already enrolled keep their second factor at sign-in. SuiteCRM core has no setting that requires a second factor, so `login.method.2fa.enforced` only offers it here."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** `services.wordpress.login.method` declares `sso`, `native` and `registration` (see [Login Methods](../../docs/contributing/design/iam/login.md)). `sso` enables the `daggerhart-openid-connect-generic` addon. With `sso` on, `native` sets the addon's `login_type`: `auto` (the default, `wp-login.php` redirects to Keycloak) while `native` is off, `button` (form plus Keycloak button) while it is on; without `sso` the WordPress form is the only sign-in. `registration` sets `users_can_register`, or the network option `registration` (`user` or `none`) under Multisite, through WP-CLI in [`tasks/07_manager_ops.yml`](./tasks/07_manager_ops.yml). WordPress core has no second factor and the role no `ldap` service, so neither is declared."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** `services.xwiki.login.method` declares `sso`, `ldap`, `native` and `registration`. `sso` renders `security.authentication.authService=oidc` into `xwiki.properties`, so the login action hands every visitor to Keycloak. `ldap` renders `authService=ldap`, which only applies while OIDC is off. `native` sets `xwiki.authentication.ldap.trylocal` in LDAP mode, so with its default `false` only directory accounts and the built-in `superadmin` pass the form; without SSO and LDAP the standard XWiki form serves the local accounts. `registration` keeps or removes a global rights object that denies the `register` right to `XWiki.XWikiGuest` (`tasks/08_registration.yml`), which hides the Register entry and refuses `XWiki.XWikiRegister`. **Break-glass:** with SSO on, `/bin/login/XWiki/XWikiLogin?oidc.skipped=true` opens the local form (the OIDC authenticator honours it while `oidc.tryLocal` keeps its default `true`), and `superadmin` with `credentials.superadminpassword` signs in there or through REST basic auth."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods:** `services.zammad.login.method.<method>.enabled` in [meta/services.yml](./meta/services.yml) declares `sso`, `ldap`, `native`, `2fa` and `registration`, and [files/ruby/apply/login_methods.rb](./files/ruby/apply/login_methods.rb) writes them into Zammad's settings at every deploy: `sso` sets `auth_openid_connect`, `native` or `ldap` sets `user_show_password_login`, `registration` sets `user_create_account`, and `2fa` sets the authenticator app and security key methods; while it is on, `login.method.2fa.enforced` (default `true`) puts every active role into `two_factor_authentication_enforce_role_ids`, and `enforced: false` empties the list so the second factor is only offered; while `2fa` is off both methods are off, so an enforcement is dormant (`Auth::TwoFactor#user_setup_required?` starts with `enabled? &&`), and the deploy empties the list, also one an admin set, so it does not wake up once an admin enables a method again. One form serves local and LDAP accounts and Zammad cannot tell them apart, so the form is shared: with `native` off in the LDAP mode a local account still signs in through it. The LDAP import keeps running with the `ldap` service, because it carries the role mapping of the directory users. Break-glass: with the password form hidden, the sign-in page links `#admin_password_auth`, which mails an administrator a one-time password-login link; `POST /api/v1/signin` keeps accepting the local administrator accounts (the API bot of `ZAMMAD_API_BOT_USER`, the wizard administrator), as does Basic auth on the REST API."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login recovery:** the profile keeps the LDAP root DN in its `Admins` list. When the search login fails, sign in to the LAM configuration editor with the LAM master password and switch the login method of the `lam` profile back to \"Fixed list\"."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login recovery:** the profile keeps the LDAP root DN in its ``Admins`` list. When the search login fails, sign in to the LAM configuration editor with the LAM master password and switch the login method of the ``lam`` profile back to “Fixed list”."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login recovery:** when the header login fails, deploy with the ``sso`` service disabled, or replace ``fdLoginMethod`` of ``cn=config,ou=fusiondirectory,<base DN>`` with ``LoginPost``, to get the login form back."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login recovery:** when the header login fails, deploy with the `sso` service disabled, or replace `fdLoginMethod` of `cn=config,ou=fusiondirectory,<base DN>` with `LoginPost`, to get the login form back."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Logo and title override semantics.** `design.logo` defaults to `assets/img/logo.png`, `design.title` defaults to `true`, which resolves to the role title (README H1). Set in `web-svc-design`, a value applies to every role. Set in a role's own `design:` entry, it applies to that role only. `false` or `0` disables the replacement for that scope. The legacy keys `title`, `titel`, `sitename` and `site_name` are migrated, the hardcoded titles in `web-app-joomla` and `web-app-pretix` are removed, and `web-app-decidim` and `web-app-socialhome` read the resolved title instead of `defaults_*`."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Logo generator.** An SVG template renders the lockup, Pillow rasterises PNG and ICO variants. The variant is computed from the target slot format: square slot shows the logo only, wide slot shows logo and title, very wide or two-line slot adds the domain, text-only slot shows the title only. The background stays transparent. The text uses palette colors with an outline so it stands out on light and dark surfaces; the SVG of a slot on a page or panel switches to the dark palette colors under `prefers-color-scheme: dark`. A slot declared with `frame: true` sits on brand-colored chrome and takes the frame text color with a frame-colored outline."
msgstr ""

